ence":0.955,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6ed6bd546dbc3102ae79315c217f8b1a1656886eb69bf62bbc4160fd6ed8ccf4","compact":"b28e12ed1bd986ff9cdccb6dcbef74530ecb671ccccad8dfa6834f0394e7d107","nasal":"30d9d6ceff2e1910b8e0275106f82e65ad7797a810f4c378c1ac5de2a3979201"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":7,"english":"O child, it behove you not to take to heart any unkind action that, O son, may have been done to you of high fame either by your mother or myself.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"51e8c0c1d784c800a30483798dbf14c36943af333c3a06186822303e3925ed43","compact":"444dfedab0111c7c7125e4313d5e09fe7eb438d33b0c430a54fd53ff329fe2c0","nasal":"444dfedab0111c7c7125e4313d5e09fe7eb438d33b0c430a54fd53ff329fe2c0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":8,"english":"You are the resource of these helpless ones; you are the eyes of these bereft of their sight. was Our lives are bound up with you. Why do not answer?'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d078dbcb8fb9dd8b0611f6792f79fe6de23cfc135ca38daf5722d35c8085e9dd","compact":"e9e2804bc8325213903cfcdf985f0f25970ca29af540f9ebddbc135531e4e6a3","nasal":"1e9c83bbc8ecee303b3c169a618ae05b35dd07b94545cb06a4ed4cdf17dcc44f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":9,"english":"Seeing the ascetic with a choked utterance indistinctly speaking thus with the letters not articulated clearly, I, dashed in spirits, yet concealing the real state of my mind by assuming a doughty tongue, communicated to him the danger that had befallen him in consequence of the calamity of his son:","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.836,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"31b024f34a312d04d95ae93b3f3fe650c20faf458a23d1212a6ad9ad6d7d58b4","compact":"3ca35bb25cc971885ea96b5a1a4f10873f2fc718cc82e6a65c613b01f827b592","nasal":"b41042d4ec37e4a66c32c61069e7c58efddee9616f99537772f04c4b485cab87"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":10,"english":"Seeing the ascetic with a choked utterance indistinctly speaking thus with the letters not articulated clearly, I, dashed in spirits, yet concealing the real state of my mind by assuming a doughty tongue, communicated to him the danger that had befallen him in consequence of the calamity of his son:","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.885,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"784ff4581f1b9698c5c35034c503d0e59185fa1c7b9e5b75093bbece1bf3a9e5","compact":"53b76765ec33d38dce82807c8ac228ca4e124bfd642d32d19c38c14fcbe4b10b","nasal":"53b76765ec33d38dce82807c8ac228ca4e124bfd642d32d19c38c14fcbe4b10b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":11,"english":"I am a Ksatriya, Dasaratha (by name), and no son of yours, O magnanimous one. I have come by a misfortune in consequence of an act blamed by the good.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"68400c3191a333d2b3cc0f86e786a6a3d7534424de3309b4f2d434b499dfcae5","compact":"540c6e45c337494213edf71d5800b0571bab179e8b158aeb7461ddaffc71989e","nasal":"540c6e45c337494213edf71d5800b0571bab179e8b158aeb7461ddaffc71989e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":12,"english":"O revered one, desirous of killing some beast of prey, and elephant (or some other), come to the waters, I went to the banks of the Sarayū bow in hand.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.862,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"81cd32f3d167092120539a3cbea2ce94359e82e339f196799438ddd01b321c18","compact":"0ef02f6f25f327bb1084d3e242d471b70b6ffc614587678f9c92773c006a25a6","nasal":"03dd1e785c6e5a73c1d4431e5336440022d3679959fd4fa10a16efaa79d8a856"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":13,"english":"Then hearing sounds from the water of a filling pitcher, I thought, This must be an elephant. I wounded it with a shaft. Next coming to the edge of the river, I saw an ascetic lying down on the ground almost deprived of life, with his heart pierced with an arrow.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.803,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1bb5427655b4e0ff428b7646860a7a9cc450dff03792e4606a6514e3c9015092","compact":"b28e621666b2e78e1fe769e21501e94ffe4b14c2aedb74d99bab8ff6bd1ae576","nasal":"03d36f1e4231cddff2c8809bada9aa4cec85bcf6f9c3dc6691dddacb2f74fe46"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":14,"english":"Then hearing sounds from the water of a filling pitcher, I thought, This must be an elephant. I wounded it with a shaft. Next coming to the edge of the river, I saw an ascetic lying down on the ground almost deprived of life, with his heart pierced with an arrow.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.887,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"eccf6f6351ac15661bce67e72f6d30094f946e2117cb134f58b11d8a0cc5065d","compact":"155d43079edeef92d94e86ffa771f813e0f6359ea3cb00cd1aaa39252303c615","nasal":"9cd48639ae5455bdbe842d032d3ce97f57dd86d3c33e76247422d77098015f9e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":15,"english":"At present for impareing me pain, Kaikeyi intoxicated by the tide of good fortune, will afflict Kausalya and Sumitrā.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.421,"method":"fuzzy-nearby-witness","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-53-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2748ec4db75dc659ca92d6fdd6b3c7a8e612f6a1f7483a4450336a058318d6c0","compact":"0758fe8c5f08bd0cf9e0853425e2478e00c595a3f6d7002968d22fe8884fadef","nasal":"b05708e86eda256d491abb2d933d05f53d127538a7a8584f8c218c388e060ff6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":16,"english":"And as soon as the arrow had been extracted, he ascended heaven, O reverend sir, lamenting and bewailing you, both grown old.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.783,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"01652f834e5cc4482aa58d0d886e837cfb09e7e4ec27d52485ef2894f4bb9927","compact":"7519e4bfa0955625013b0a2805b73cfe4bf91f6d9b8db958f198f7cd7b19be72","nasal":"1a1d35cd93fbc4a44561d7ba7f766b1647be715f3b78058df9e2ab73d60476ff"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":17,"english":"It is through ignorance that I suddenly wounded your son. This having been past, do you favour me with telling me what is now to be done, O ascetic.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b8421b8175693ca542eca330a48d1369cf06a0b111ead8b6041ba62e117f3c56","compact":"9f8f1a72ccd0b9edde9db3d12d891145b87111ea9c001c2905186d7d1e3bcec8","nasal":"9f8f1a72ccd0b9edde9db3d12d891145b87111ea9c001c2905186d7d1e3bcec8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":18,"english":"With eyes flooded with tears, and well nigh deprived of his senses by grief, that highly energetic one said to me standing with joined hands.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.448,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c2bc6b3c19331f0c7279a30e1e56348cfa7f2c447b104e5cdc52ee1e776803a0","compact":"def5677de3b5b1130f331a9578a8c74b6d983809d118c7308bb0a871bc02b677","nasal":"43449ee9f6059ba48d01422783e1a82aeaecff48b2714b92d3f2604bd18fa41f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":19,"english":"If, O king, you had not of yourself immediately communicated to us this unpleasant news, your head would have been reduced to a thousand flaws.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.85,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b1bebe307d2fd70528e216266fb712bd56f6326bebf152980ecbcb4f7ea2eb09","compact":"bec8cb9b9dcfe9c80d1236fa9de0a55811f99535b8e9517f800ae80a580a0c89","nasal":"e67c5153c00c4c4f994e8cbbc6f3370baa9f4726cbfaf3ea1b22afa7a0850f3a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":20,"english":"Not to speak of Kștriyas, I can even drag the very wielder of the thunderbolt himself from his position, if he knowingly kills one, in especial, that has assumed the Vånaprastha mode of life.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2b87530d7ee72726603d1faa92ec8e61b7fa7570a709b145b66bfab8a04850d0","compact":"b1ce631c4537dac5be12e549d615a085053b809e923fca40d654156720eeb7b4","nasal":"b1ce631c4537dac5be12e549d615a085053b809e923fca40d654156720eeb7b4"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":21,"english":"It is because you have done this through ignorance that you (still) live, else the race itself of the Rāghavas should be not, and where are you?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.594,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"31083383a160ef774de12177ec27a1a208c071a7293eb655b78d5672d6db15dd","compact":"74d706c9b78e51e57dd77bb9c23661958e5a09c0250c12972af297a19e112dd8","nasal":"74d706c9b78e51e57dd77bb9c23661958e5a09c0250c12972af297a19e112dd8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":22,"english":"He then said, 'Do you, o king, take us to the scene. To day will we look our last on our son besmeared with blood, his deer-skin garb falling off (from his body), lying senseless on the earth, and come under the subjection of the lord of righteousness.'","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"009fc02000601baa7bb199957a2edf2b5bd804b4fb97c1806e30af034ad863bf","compact":"56766f0b0d28d0d12345cc4fe13f0eae5c4e6490dbbfffb4813845fe2c0104e1","nasal":"56766f0b0d28d0d12345cc4fe13f0eae5c4e6490dbbfffb4813845fe2c0104e1"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":23,"english":"Thereupon I alone taking them exceedingly disconsolate to the spot, made the ascetic and his wife touch their son.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.883,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"23281fcff3a1557cd767edb646c04f9d79cd180ca01f0147bd49622aa423b725","compact":"ecc3cf3524387474ff5eec01306df9ed762ef88d27da38c88d4659a37e7fddf9","nasal":"75ce59e197b213d4ba17cd771bd3cecab1f61182a1ead9edffa6b5723acfd27d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":24,"english":"Thereupon I alone taking them exceedingly disconsolate to the spot, made the ascetic and his wife touch their son.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"284a7a9eda18d338d503025b0b0c3d7889459a0a910fe4dce7f3de388a3ca6de","compact":"24b7bc9f52d0a3d2795468506a5cbc4b1b3fbc4bfeb44da6d301c183cb8f3962","nasal":"24b7bc9f52d0a3d2795468506a5cbc4b1b3fbc4bfeb44da6d301c183cb8f3962"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":25,"english":"And having approached their son and touched him, ascetics fell on his person, and then his father addressed him thus.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.794,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"88c447e84479e88b90c2664cd7b8444e47211353e901643e045a2844ec729a76","compact":"321d2f4b3721b7a00b52372d00ea7293ed88d1f1728a1c4b1f714f466cd9c68e","nasal":"321d2f4b3721b7a00b52372d00ea7293ed88d1f1728a1c4b1f714f466cd9c68e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":26,"english":"If, my son, you do not feel kindly to me, do you look up to your virtuous mother. And why, O son, do you not embrace me? Do you speak tender words.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.797,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058026","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7828db45f9ae88ab2a40ff9db2ca15ee59c064616330b24417bdb7512140e544","compact":"fce40a22976adb2c86b1ea551df0a9557a292a8253bc1fa574fc125708bc696a","nasal":"b266d0f46f7e79a4fdf770c86355fd393512d61bf0fd5bf0c81613cd409deb07"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-58-27","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":58,"verse":27,"english":"At the small hours, form whom engaged in study, shall I hear the scriptures sweetly read in a way coming home to the listener's mind?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-64-28"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2058027","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6f3c0f8d6f2725daf8576f2a91be47aae3860330da8130482baead15a5471452","compact":"b0e16eeba3b726be09c06741a5b442c51